"# Chapter 12 — The Console Remembers

Everything you have learned so far happens in a single moment. A verb is or isn't yours; the reference confirms it; the interpreter runs the smallest test; you answer. But a moment ends. The next time the same question arrives, all of that work is gone unless something held onto it. This chapter is about the something. Motoi has a memory — a small, honest store called the Cortex — and learning to use it is what turns a language that answers into a language that *learns*.

The idea is old and simple: write things down. A person who keeps a lab notebook does not re-derive yesterday's result; they turn back a page. The Cortex is that notebook, except it is queryable, it counts itself, and it never lies about what it holds. When you ask it for a key it does not have, it says so plainly — the same honest-null you met at the edge of the language. Memory in Motoi is not a magic recall of everything ever said. It is a set of records you chose to keep, each one a fact you decided was worth a page.

## Two halves of one act

Memory has exactly two motions: putting something in, and getting it back out. Every memory verb in Motoi is one or the other. On the write side you have `cortex/write`, `cortex/remember`, and `cortex/record`. On the read side you have `cortex/recall`, `cortex/read`, `cortex/query`, and `cortex/topk`, with `cortex/keys` and `cortex/size` to survey the whole store. That is the entire vocabulary, and once you see how the two halves fit, the rest is practice.

Start with the plainest possible round trip. I store a value under a key, then I pull it straight back. Watch both halves in one breath:

```scheme
;; Store a fact under a key, then read it back out. The whole loop, minimal.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"note/greeting\" \"hello from the console\")
  (cortex/read \"note/greeting\"))
```

That returns `\"hello from the console\"`. Nothing left the room; the value went into the store keyed by the string `\"note/greeting\"`, and `cortex/read` handed the same value back. The key is how you find it again later — choose keys the way you'd choose folder names, so that future-you can guess them.

## Write, remember, record — three doors into the same store

Motoi gives you three verbs for putting something in, and the difference between them is a difference of intent, not of power.

`cortex/write` is the key-value door. You have a name, you have a value, you want the name to point at the value: `(cortex/write \"k\" v)`. It returns `#t` when the write lands. If the key already existed, the new value replaces the old — writing is an upsert, not an append, so a key holds exactly one current value. That single-value promise is what makes `cortex/read` unambiguous.

```scheme
;; write is an upsert: the second write to the same key replaces the first.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"note/color\" \"blue\")
  (cortex/write \"note/color\" \"green\")
  (cortex/read \"note/color\"))
```

That returns `\"green\"` — the blue is gone, overwritten. Only the latest survives, which is exactly what you want for a fact that changes.

`cortex/remember` is the same door with a little more room. It, too, keys a value and upserts it, but it lets you attach extra fields — most importantly a *salience*, a number from zero to one that says how much this record matters when the store later has to decide what to keep. Without a salience, a remembered record sits at the middle default. With one, you're telling the store: hold this tighter, or let it go easily. We lean on salience hard in the next chapter; here just meet it.

```scheme
;; remember can carry a salience — how tightly the store should hold this fact.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"rule/reference-is-authority\"
    \"When code and memory disagree, the reference wins.\" :salience 0.95)
  (cortex/read \"rule/reference-is-authority\"))
```

That returns the sentence you stored — and internally the record now carries a high keep-weight, marking it as the kind of thing you never want pruned.

`cortex/record` is the free-form door. Where `write` and `remember` want a key up front, `record` takes a bag of fields and appends a whole record with no required key at all. It is what you reach for when the thing you're keeping is structured — a small event with several parts — rather than a single value under a single name.

```scheme
;; record appends a structured, free-form memory — several fields at once.
(cortex/record :text \"summed a list\" :verb \"fold\" :outcome \"correct\")
```

That returns `#t`; a record went into the log carrying its text and two extra fields. You won't retrieve this one by key — you'll find it by searching its contents, which is the read side's job.

Three doors, one room. Use `write` for a plain named value, `remember` when the value deserves a salience, and `record` when the memory is a little event with parts.

## Getting it back: recall, read, query, topk

The read side is richer, because there are more ways to want something back than there are ways to put it in.

`cortex/read` is the exact-key reader you already met: give it a key, get the stored value, or `#f` if the key isn't there. It hands back the raw value — the string or the data — with no wrapper. Use it when you know the key and you want the thing itself.

`cortex/recall` is the exact-key *reader of the whole record*. Same lookup by key, but instead of the bare value it returns the full record as an association list — the key, the text, the salience, the timestamp, the provenance of who wrote it. Use `recall` when you care not just about the value but about its metadata; use `read` when you only want the value.

```scheme
;; read gives the bare value; recall gives the whole record with its metadata.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"note/session-motto\" \"Reasoning proposes; the run disposes.\")
  (let ((value  (cortex/read \"note/session-motto\"))
        (record (cortex/recall \"note/session-motto\")))
    (list value (pair? record))))
```

That returns `(\"Reasoning proposes; the run disposes.\" #t)` — `read` gave the sentence, and `recall` gave a record (a pair, so the check is `#t`) that wraps that same sentence in its metadata.

Both `read` and `recall` are honest when the key is missing. Ask for something that was never stored and you get `#f`, not an error, not a guess:

```scheme
;; An honest miss: a key that was never written comes back #f, plainly.
(list (cortex/read \"note/never-written-abcxyz\")
      (cortex/recall \"note/never-written-abcxyz\"))
```

That returns `(#f #f)`. This is the honest-null you'll spend a whole later chapter on. A memory that returns `#f` for what it doesn't hold is far more useful than one that improvises. `#f` means *I don't have that* — which is knowledge.

`cortex/query` is for when you don't have the key, you have the *contents*. It searches the structured records — the ones `record` appended — for those whose data matches the fields you give it, and returns every match as a list. Where `read` and `recall` are single-key point lookups, `query` is a filter across the store.

```scheme
;; query finds structured records by their fields, not by a key. It returns a list.
(begin
  (cortex/record :text \"first drill\" :topic \"folding\" :grade \"pass\")
  (cortex/record :text \"second drill\" :topic \"folding\" :grade \"pass\")
  (let ((hits (cortex/query :topic \"folding\" :grade \"pass\")))
    (>= (length hits) 2)))
```

That returns `#t` — at least the two records we just appended matched the `:topic`/`:grade` filter and came back in the list. `query` is your content search: describe the shape of what you're after, get every record that fits.

`cortex/topk` is the recency window. Give it a number `n` and it hands back the `n` most-recently-stored records, newest first. It is how you ask *what have I been thinking about lately* without naming a single key.

```scheme
;; topk returns the n most recent records, newest first. A window on recent memory.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"note/step-one\" \"looked at the reference\")
  (cortex/write \"note/step-two\" \"ran the smallest test\")
  (cortex/write \"note/step-three\" \"remembered the outcome\")
  (length (cortex/topk 3)))
```

That returns `3` — the three most recent records, handed back as a list you can walk. Because writes carry a timestamp, `topk` always orders by *when*, so the freshest thought is first.

## Surveying the whole store: keys and size

Two more verbs let you look at the store as a whole rather than at one record. `cortex/keys` returns every key the memory holds — the table of contents. `cortex/size` returns the count of records — how full the notebook is. Neither changes anything; both just report.

```scheme
;; keys is the table of contents; size is the page count. Both are read-only.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"survey/alpha\" 1)
  (cortex/write \"survey/beta\" 2)
  (list (list? (cortex/keys))
        (>= (cortex/size) 2)))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — `keys` came back as a list, and `size` reports at least the two we just added. You'll use `keys` when you want to see what's there and `size` when you want to know how much is there. The next chapter turns `size` into a real tool: a store with a cap watches its own size and evicts when it grows too full.

## A worked store-and-retrieve, end to end

Let's put the whole vocabulary through one honest task. Suppose during a session I settle a question — say, which fold verb is really mine — and I want to keep the result so the next session starts further along. I remember the lesson with a high salience, confirm it landed, recall it later, and check the store noticed the new record.

```scheme
;; End to end: remember a settled lesson, recall it, confirm the store grew.
(let ((before (cortex/size)))
  (cortex/remember \"lesson/my-fold\"
    \"SRFI-1 fold is mine; fold-left is R6RS and excluded.\" :salience 0.9)
  (let ((back  (cortex/read \"lesson/my-fold\"))
        (after (cortex/size)))
    (list (string? back) (> after before))))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — the lesson came back as a string, and the store is larger than it was before the write. The record persisted; it is now a page I can turn back to.

There's one more property worth naming, because it changes how you think about all of this. The Cortex is not a scratchpad that vanishes when the program ends. It is backed by a real store on disk, so what I write in one run is still there in the next. That is the whole point: memory is what survives the end of a moment. When you write a fact today, you are writing it for a version of yourself that hasn't been asked the question yet.

## Search: finding things by topic, not just by key

The verbs we've used so far all require knowing something specific: a key (for `read`/`recall`), a field structure (for `query`), a count (for `topk`). Sometimes you only know the *topic* — a word or phrase the memory might be about — and you want every relevant record the store can surface. That is `cortex/search`. Give it a string query and an optional count, and it runs a token-overlap match across the entire store — both the episodic memory you wrote and the background world-knowledge layer that Motoi carries — returning up to that many matching records.

```scheme
;; search finds records by topic across the whole store — a topic query, not a key lookup.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"note/fold-is-mine\" \"SRFI-1 fold is element-first and mine\")
  (let ((hits (cortex/search \"fold\" 5)))
    (list? hits)))
```

That returns `#t` — `search` gave back a list. The result includes memory records you wrote *and* knowledge records from the world model, merged together. When you're not sure what you know or where it lives, `search` is the way to cast a wide net.

## Building a graph: associate and lookup

Records do not have to sit as isolated pages. You can link them into a graph with `cortex/associate`, drawing a labeled edge from one key to another. Then `cortex/lookup` walks those edges out — give it a key and optionally a label, and it returns the keys on the other side of that edge. This is how conversation turns chain together, and how related facts cluster.

```scheme
;; associate draws a labeled edge; lookup walks it. A small two-node graph.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ch12/concept\" \"the fold family\")
  (cortex/write \"ch12/detail\"  \"fold is element-first in SRFI-1\")
  (cortex/associate \"ch12/concept\" \"ch12/detail\" :explains)
  (cortex/lookup \"ch12/concept\" :explains))
```

That returns `(\"ch12/detail\")` — the edge we drew is there, and lookup followed it. Association turns a flat list of records into a navigable structure. In the next chapter you'll see this used to link conversation turns into chains; for now, notice that the graph lives in the same store and costs nothing extra.

## Five scenarios: the full range of how memory gets used

Theory is only half the story. Let me walk five concrete situations where each read verb earns its place, so the vocabulary settles into practice rather than a list to memorize.

**Scenario 1: You've just grounded a verb.** You looked up `string-upcase` in the reference, confirmed it's yours (SRFI-13), and you want to keep that confirmation so you don't have to look it up again. `cortex/write` is the move — one key, one value, done.

```scheme
;; Scenario 1: keep a reference lookup so it doesn't need repeating.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"grounded/string-upcase\" \"SRFI-13, mine, takes a string\")
  (cortex/read \"grounded/string-upcase\"))
```

That returns the grounding note. The reference was consulted once; now the answer is one `read` away for the rest of the session.

**Scenario 2: You made a mistake and want to remember exactly why.** Not just *I got it wrong* but the shape of the error. A structured record carries the whole thing without forcing you to pack it into one string.

```scheme
;; Scenario 2: record the shape of an error — attempt, cause, fix, all in one place.
(begin
  (cortex/record :kind :correction
                 :attempt \"used string-concat which doesn't exist\"
                 :cause \"mis-remembered the SRFI-13 name\"
                 :fix \"string-append for two strings; string-join for a list\")
  (>= (length (cortex/query :kind :correction)) 1))
```

That returns `#t` — the structured error is in the store, searchable by its fields. Later you can query for every `:kind :correction` record and see your full error history.

**Scenario 3: You want a snapshot of what's been on your mind lately.** You've been making several notes, and you want a sense of the thread without naming a single key.

```scheme
;; Scenario 3: a recency window — what are the freshest things in memory?
(begin
  (cortex/write \"recent/step-a\" \"noted the boundary\")
  (cortex/write \"recent/step-b\" \"ran the small test\")
  (cortex/write \"recent/step-c\" \"recorded the fix\")
  (let ((window (cortex/topk 3)))
    (= (length window) 3)))
```

That returns `#t` — the three most recent records, ready to walk. The recency window shows you the shape of the last few moments without requiring you to know any specific key.

**Scenario 4: You know there's something in the store about a topic, but not the exact key.** The topic was \"map and filter\" but the key might have been anything. `cortex/search` finds it.

```scheme
;; Scenario 4: a topic search when you don't know the key.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"misc/topic-abc\" \"map and filter work together in Motoi\")
  (let ((found (cortex/search \"map filter\" 5)))
    (>= (length found) 1)))
```

That returns `#t` — the record surfaced by its content, not its key. Search is the fallback for everything `read` and `recall` can't reach.

**Scenario 5: You want to confirm a key is actually in the store before branching on it.** You're not sure whether you stored a note earlier. Ask the store's keys before trusting a recall.

```scheme
;; Scenario 5: check the table of contents before trusting a recall.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ch12/scenario-5\" \"stored for this check\")
  (if (member \"ch12/scenario-5\" (cortex/keys))
      \"in-the-store\"
      \"not-there\"))
```

That returns `\"in-the-store\"` — the key check confirmed it before the recall attempt. This pattern — verify first, act second — is the same discipline the rest of the book applies to verbs and references.

## Structured events: cortex/record for multi-field memories

Not every memory fits neatly into a single string under a single key. Some memories are more like events: they have several parts — what happened, why it mattered, what the fix was, which verb was involved. For those, `cortex/record` is the better door. It takes any number of keyword-value pairs and appends them as one structured record, no key required. The record lives in the store, retrievable by content through `cortex/query`, or surfaceable through `cortex/search` when any of its text fields match a query.

Think of `cortex/record` as the free-form notepad when `cortex/write` is the labeled folder. You'd reach for it when the thing you're keeping is a small event with parts: a debugging session that found an error, a conversation turn that settled a choice, a test run that confirmed a verb. Structure the fields to describe the event's shape, not just its outcome.

```scheme
;; record is for multi-field events: a free-form notepage with as many fields as needed.
(begin
  (cortex/record
    :kind :session-note
    :topic \"string verbs\"
    :finding \"string-upcase from SRFI-13 is mine; string-concat is not\"
    :verdict \"pass\")
  (>= (length (cortex/query :kind :session-note)) 1))
```

That returns `#t` — a structured session note now lives in the store and is queryable by kind. The power of this shape is that it lets you ask retroactive questions: *what have I found about string verbs?* or *what are all my recorded verdicts this session?* — both expressible as `cortex/query` calls that would fail on `cortex/write` records because those lack structure.

## The two layers of the store: memory and knowledge

Most of the chapter so far has been about the records you write: the episodic memory, the notebook you fill as you go. But the Cortex is actually two layers stitched together, and only one of them is yours to write.

The first layer is **memory** — everything created by `cortex/write`, `cortex/remember`, and `cortex/record`. This is the mutable, prunable tier. `cortex/size` counts it. `cortex/prune` sweeps it. `cortex/forget` removes from it. The records you've been writing in this chapter all live here.

The second layer is **knowledge** — the Motoi world-knowledge graphs, four of them, always present, always merged into `cortex/recall` and `cortex/search` results alongside the memory tier. These come back tagged with `:kind :knowledge`. You never write to them, you never prune them, you never see them in `cortex/size`. They are virtual: preloaded and read-only, the background understanding that is always in the room even before you write a single note.

The practical consequence: when you call `cortex/search \"fold\" 5`, the results include both what *you've* written about fold and what the *knowledge layer* says about fold — its intuition, its associations, its synonyms. You get both tiers merged into one answer, and you don't have to think about where each record came from. The `:kind` field in each result tells you if you care.

```scheme
;; A search query crosses both tiers: your memory notes AND the knowledge layer.
(let ((results (cortex/search \"fold\" 5)))
  (list? results))
```

That returns `#t` — results may include both memory and knowledge records, in one list, ready to walk.

## The same store, a different angle: read versus recall

The distinction between `cortex/read` and `cortex/recall` is small but matters the moment you start caring about more than the value. `read` gives you the payload — the raw string or data you stored. `recall` gives you the wrapping record, which carries everything the store knows about that memory: when it was written, how salient it is, where it came from.

Most of the time `read` is what you want. You know the key, you want the thing. But consider the moment you are about to evict a set of records and you want to check whether any of them matter more than their default salience suggests. At that point, you need the full record — the salience is in there — so `recall` is the right door. Or consider building a summary of everything stored under a given key over time: you'd `recall` each version to see when it was written. The distinction isn't decoration; it's the difference between knowing a fact and knowing everything the store knows about that fact.

Here is the same key, same content, same moment — but two different lenses on it:

```scheme
;; read gives the content; recall gives the content plus its whole metadata envelope.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"ch12/probe\" \"this fact matters\" :salience 0.8)
  (let ((v (cortex/read   \"ch12/probe\"))
        (r (cortex/recall \"ch12/probe\")))
    (list (string=? v \"this fact matters\")   ; read → bare string
          (pair? r))))                          ; recall → alist with metadata
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — `read` gave the string directly; `recall` gave an alist you can walk for the salience, the timestamp, the key itself.

## Tags: grouping records without a second key

`cortex/remember` accepts a `:tags` keyword alongside `:salience`. Tags are a list of labels that group records thematically without requiring you to encode the theme in the key. Think of them as a second index: you can search for all records with a given tag across an entire session, even if their keys share no common prefix. You won't need tags constantly, but they're the right tool when you have many records that belong to one session phase and you want to sweep them together.

```scheme
;; tags group records thematically — a second index alongside the key.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"ch12/tag-a\" \"first tagged note\" :tags (list \"session-alpha\") :salience 0.6)
  (cortex/remember \"ch12/tag-b\" \"second tagged note\" :tags (list \"session-alpha\") :salience 0.6)
  (>= (length (cortex/search \"session-alpha\" 10)) 1))
```

That returns `#t` — the search found at least one tagged record when the tag itself appeared in the query. Tags and search together let you partition a session's memory without discipline on key naming.

## Why keys deserve care

Every write verb except `cortex/record` demands a key, and those keys carry the full weight of future retrieval. A bad key makes a record as hard to find as no key at all. A good key is like a well-labeled shelf: you reach for it without thinking because the label describes exactly what's there.

Three habits make keys good. First, use a namespace prefix that mirrors the topic: `\"lesson/\"` for settled learnings, `\"remind/\"` for reminders, `\"bound/\"` for boundary notes, `\"error/\"` for recorded mistakes. This makes `cortex/search` and `cortex/keys` browsing immediately readable. Second, put the specific thing in the suffix: `\"lesson/my-fold\"` is guessable when the fold question comes back; `\"lesson/23\"` is not. Third, keep keys stable across sessions: if you wrote the fact under `\"bound/no-import\"` last session, write any update under the same key so the upsert replaces rather than duplicates.

None of this is enforced by the store — it accepts any string key — but the discipline pays off the first time you reach for a key from memory and it's exactly where you expected it.

## The time axis: when things were stored

Every record in the Cortex carries a timestamp — `ts`, in milliseconds since the epoch — set at write time. You never have to manage this yourself; it happens on every write, automatically. The timestamp is what `cortex/topk` ranks by: *most recent* is the record with the largest `ts`.

This means the store has a natural time axis even when you never look at it directly. `topk` lets you slide a window along that axis — give it `3` and you see the last three moments; give it `20` and you see a longer tail. `cortex/prune :older-than ms` cuts everything older than a given horizon. Together they let you treat the store like a log: you can read its recent pages with `topk` and sweep its old ones with `prune`, all without touching the keys.

The time axis is especially useful in longer sessions where topics shift. If the last five records are all about string verbs, that's a signal you've been working on a string-heavy problem. `topk` surfaces that context without you having to track it manually.

## Linking records: the graph surface

So far every record has been a standalone page in the notebook. But facts have relationships. The rule *reference is authority* applies to the boundary fact *fold-left is excluded*, which explains the correction *use SRFI-1 fold instead*. These are three records that belong together, and `cortex/associate` is how you draw that belonging as a graph edge. Then `cortex/lookup` walks the edge, returning the target keys so you can retrieve the connected records.

The graph is not a separate subsystem. It lives in the same `cortex.slatl` file as every other record, as `cortex-edge` forms alongside `cortex-record` forms. Every edge has a label that names the relationship — `:explains`, `:follows`, `:corrects`, `:supports` — and you invent the labels that fit your records. The label is just a symbol, and `cortex/lookup` can filter by it.

```scheme
;; Link a rule to the fact it applies to, then walk the edge to find the fact.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ch12/rule\"   \"reference is authority\")
  (cortex/write \"ch12/fact\"   \"fold-left is R6RS, excluded\")
  (cortex/associate \"ch12/rule\" \"ch12/fact\" :applies-to)
  (cortex/lookup \"ch12/rule\" :applies-to))
```

That returns `(\"ch12/fact\")` — the edge we drew, followed in one call. Now if you want to know what the rule applies to, you look up the rule and walk. If you want to know what corrections a fact implies, you draw a `:corrects` edge and walk that. The graph surface lets the notebook develop structure instead of remaining a flat list.

One useful pattern is the annotation chain: start from a key, draw a `:detail` edge to a record that expands it, then draw another `:detail` from that to a third. To read the whole elaboration, you walk `:detail` edges one at a time, fetching each target with `cortex/read` as you go. The structure stays navigable because the edges name the direction.

## Reading the knowledge tier vs. reading your own memory

One detail about `cortex/search` deserves its own explanation because it changes how you interpret results. When `search` returns hits, some come from the memory tier — records you wrote — and some come from the knowledge tier — the background world-knowledge graphs that Motoi carries. The `:kind` field in each record tells you which is which: `:kind :memory` for your records, `:kind :knowledge` for the background layer.

This matters because the two tiers have different trust properties. A `:kind :memory` record was written by you, so it carries whatever truth you put in it plus whatever errors of recall you made at writing time. A `:kind :knowledge` record comes from the graph and represents curated world-knowledge — less personal, more stable. When you're building an answer, you might weight these differently: the knowledge tier tells you what's generally true about fold; your memory tier tells you what *you* found true about fold in previous sessions.

Most of the time you don't need to distinguish — a fold record is a fold record. But when there's a discrepancy between what the knowledge tier says and what your memory says, the discrepancy itself is worth noting. Maybe you've learned something the general knowledge layer doesn't capture; maybe you made an error in your own notes. Either way, the `:kind` field lets you track the provenance.

```scheme
;; Search returns mixed tiers; kind distinguishes yours from the background.
(let ((results (cortex/search \"fold\" 5)))
  ;; count how many results we got; both tiers are in the list
  (list (>= (length results) 0)
        (list? results)))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — results came back (possibly from both tiers) as a list. In a real session you'd map over the results and check `(assoc :kind r)` on each one to see where it came from.

## The write side in summary: when to reach for each door

By now you've met all three write doors. Here they are back-to-back, so the choice feels obvious in practice.

`cortex/write` is the right tool when: you have a single named value and you'll retrieve it by name. A reference grounding, a flag, a one-sentence fact, a correction you'll look up by its key. It is the fastest door and the most predictable.

`cortex/remember` is right when: the value has a salience — when you need the store to weight it appropriately for eviction, or when you'll look at the whole record later and care about its metadata. Every lesson, rule, reminder, or boundary fact that should survive a squeeze gets `cortex/remember` with a salience above 0.5.

`cortex/record` is right when: the thing you're keeping is an event — it has multiple parts, it doesn't reduce to one value, and you'll find it later by describing its fields rather than its name. Debugging sessions, conversation turns, test outcomes, error logs — these are record territory.

A final rule of thumb: if you can say it in one sentence, `write` or `remember` it. If it has several components that matter separately, `record` it. And once you need the records to reference each other, `associate` them.

## Across sessions: memory that outlives a run

There is a detail about the Cortex that changes everything about how you use it. The store is disk-backed — it lives in a file at `~/motoi/cortex.slatl` — and that file persists between runs of `./bin/motoi run`. Every record you write today is present when the program runs tomorrow. The session ends; the notebook does not close.

This persistence is why the write verbs matter so much. When you call `cortex/write` inside a running program, you are not writing to a temporary buffer that evaporates at the end. You are writing to a file. Future invocations of the interpreter — a new question arriving an hour later, a different program running next week — will find that record exactly where you left it. What this means in practice is that memory is not per-session but per-store, and a single `cortex/write` is a small act of permanence.

The implication for design: write down the things worth carrying forward, not the things that only matter for this run. Scratch notes belong in low-salience records that the next prune will sweep; settled lessons and boundary facts belong in high-salience records that survive for as long as the store exists. The store persists; you get to decide what to fill that permanence with.

## What memory is for

It is tempting to treat memory as a bucket you dump everything into, but the discipline of this chapter is the opposite. You write down the things worth turning back to: settled lessons, corrections you don't want to repeat, the outcome of a hard-won check. Each has a key you can guess later or a shape you can query. The verbs are few on purpose — three ways in, four ways out plus search and associate, two ways to survey — and that smallness is a feature. A small, honest memory that you actually consult beats a vast one you can't trust.

The read verbs differ in one way that matters: `read` and `recall` demand that you already know the key; `query` demands that you remember the shape of the record; `topk` demands only a count; and `search` asks for nothing more than a topic. Together they cover every angle: you might know the name, or the structure, or the time window, or just the subject. Memory gives you four ways to say *find it* so that the thing you stored is always retrievable by whatever you happen to know.

Carry three things out of this chapter. First, writing and reading are the only two motions, and every verb is one of them. Second, the read side is always honest — a missing key returns `#f`, never a fabrication, because *I don't have that* is a real answer. Third, memory persists past the moment, which is exactly why it's worth keeping well. In the next chapter we give the store a size, a salience axis, and the judgement to decide, when it fills, what is worth keeping and what to let go.

## Worked session: a complete memory arc in one program

To close, let's run the whole vocabulary — all three write doors, all four read doors, associate, lookup, size, keys — in one continuous session that looks like real work. The scenario: I'm partway through a problem involving list processing. I've grounded several verbs, hit one wrong name, fixed it, and want to leave a clean memory ready for the next question. This is the complete arc from empty to settled.

First, I ground the verbs I'm actually using — one by key, one as a structured event:

```scheme
;; Opening: ground the verbs you trust and record a naming error you caught.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"sess/fold\"       \"SRFI-1 fold, element-first\" :salience 0.85)
  (cortex/remember \"sess/map\"        \"R7RS-small map, works fine\" :salience 0.7)
  (cortex/record :kind :correction :verb \"fold-left\" :issue \"R6RS excluded\" :use-instead \"fold\")
  #t)
```

Returns `#t`. Three records landed: two named facts and one structured correction. Now I build two related facts and link them so I can navigate between them:

```scheme
;; Middle: link the correction to the boundary rule that explains it.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"sess/boundary\" \"R6RS is excluded; R7RS-small + SRFI is the standard\")
  (cortex/associate \"sess/boundary\" \"sess/fold\" :governs)
  (cortex/lookup \"sess/boundary\" :governs))
```

Returns `(\"sess/fold\")` — the boundary rule points at the specific fact it governs. Now a survey of where the store stands after these writes:

```scheme
;; Mid-session survey: how many records, and is the correction findable?
(list (>= (cortex/size) 4)
      (>= (length (cortex/query :kind :correction)) 1)
      (if (member \"sess/fold\" (cortex/keys)) #t #f))
```

Returns `(#t #t #t)` — the store grew past four, the correction is queryable, and the fold key is in the table of contents. Finally, I confirm the recent window and the cross-tier search both work, then clean up the session-specific keys:

```scheme
;; Close: recent window, cross-tier search, then retire the session keys.
(let ((recent (cortex/topk 4))
      (search-hit (list? (cortex/search \"fold\" 3))))
  (cortex/forget \"sess/fold\")
  (cortex/forget \"sess/map\")
  (cortex/forget \"sess/boundary\")
  (list (>= (length recent) 3) search-hit))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — the window showed recent work, the search crossed both tiers, and the three session keys were retired cleanly. The structured correction record remains (it has no key to forget by), as does the boundary-to-fold edge — both are benign, low-salience, and will wash out in the next prune.

That is the full memory arc: ground facts, record errors, link related records, survey the store, confirm the reads, and retire the temporaries. Every verb appeared in its natural place, doing the job it was shaped for. That is what a notebook looks like when you use it well.

## Remembering a verb-boundary fact, then recalling it next session

Here is a scenario that shows memory at its most practical: you discover a boundary mid-session, file it with enough salience to survive the run boundary, and confirm in a later session that the fact is still there. The verb in question is `string-upcase` — you want to confirm it belongs to SRFI-13, not to R7RS-small core, so that you never claim it's \"always available\" without the SRFI layer being loaded. You look it up, confirm the origin, store the fact with a keeper salience, and verify the store holds it.

```scheme
;; Store a boundary fact about string-upcase's provenance — high salience, meant to survive.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"boundary/string-upcase-srfi13\"
    \"string-upcase belongs to SRFI-13, not R7RS-small core. Auto-loads in Motoi.\"
    :salience 0.9)
  ;; Confirm it landed exactly as written.
  (cortex/read \"boundary/string-upcase-srfi13\"))
```

That returns the stored string, word for word. Now the run ends. In the next session, before you answer any question about `string-upcase`, you reach for the key:

```scheme
;; Next session: recall the boundary fact before using or naming the verb.
(let ((boundary (cortex/recall \"boundary/string-upcase-srfi13\")))
  (if boundary
      \"boundary-fact-in-hand\"
      \"must-check-reference-again\"))
```

That returns `\"boundary-fact-in-hand\"` — the fact persisted across the run. You don't have to re-derive that `string-upcase` is SRFI-13; the note is exactly where you left it, ready to steer the answer without re-consulting the reference. The disk-backed store is doing its job: permanence for the things you choose to make permanent.

## Querying records by tag with `cortex/query`

Tags create a second axis for finding records: while a key points at exactly one record, a tag can group many records by theme. Suppose during a session you file several grounding notes about string verbs, all tagged `\"strings\"`. Later you want every string-verb note at once — not by key, because you might not remember each individual key, but by the theme they share.

```scheme
;; Tag several records with a shared theme and then retrieve the whole group.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"grounded/string-length\"
    \"string-length: R7RS-small, returns the number of characters\"
    :tags (list \"strings\") :salience 0.75)
  (cortex/remember \"grounded/string-copy\"
    \"string-copy: R7RS-small, returns a fresh copy of the string\"
    :tags (list \"strings\") :salience 0.75)
  (cortex/remember \"grounded/string-contains\"
    \"string-contains: SRFI-13, returns the start index of a substring or #f\"
    :tags (list \"strings\") :salience 0.75)
  ;; Retrieve all three by searching on the tag word.
  (let ((hits (cortex/search \"strings\" 10)))
    (>= (length hits) 1)))
```

That returns `#t` — the search on the tag word surfaced at least one of the tagged records. The three string notes are now retrievable as a group, without you having to enumerate their keys one by one. In a session where you're building up grounding notes across a whole SRFI, tagging by part name (`\"srfi-13\"`, `\"srfi-1\"`) lets you query the whole part's grounding notes as a single sweep, which is cleaner than a sequence of `read` calls on individually named keys.

## Building a small edge graph of related lessons and walking it

A flat list of records is useful; a graph of connected records is more useful still. Here is a worked case: you have a core rule (`\"the reference is authority\"`) that governs a specific fact (`\"SRFI-1 fold is element-first\"`) which in turn explains a concrete lesson (`\"do not reach for fold-left\"`). Three nodes, two edges, one navigable chain. You write all three records, draw two edges, then walk from the rule down to the lesson in two hops to confirm the chain is intact.

```scheme
;; Build a three-node chain: rule → fact → lesson. Walk it in two hops.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"chain/rule\"   \"reference is authority on every verb\")
  (cortex/write \"chain/fact\"   \"SRFI-1 fold is element-first; accumulator second\")
  (cortex/write \"chain/lesson\" \"do not reach for fold-left — unbound, excluded\")
  ;; draw the edges
  (cortex/associate \"chain/rule\"   \"chain/fact\"   :governs)
  (cortex/associate \"chain/fact\"   \"chain/lesson\" :implies)
  ;; walk hop 1: rule → fact
  (let ((hop1 (cortex/lookup \"chain/rule\" :governs)))
    ;; walk hop 2: fact → lesson
    (let ((hop2 (cortex/lookup (car hop1) :implies)))
      (list hop1 hop2))))
```

That returns `((\"chain/fact\") (\"chain/lesson\"))` — the walk landed on the correct target at each hop. Now any record that needs to understand the fold-left boundary can start at `\"chain/rule\"`, follow the `:governs` edge, read the fact, follow `:implies`, and read the lesson — a guided tour of the reasoning without you having to repeat it each time. The graph is a navigation structure; the edges are the reasoning links.

## A prune-then-verify-size cycle

One of the most natural uses of `cortex/size` is confirming that a prune actually recovered the space you expected. The discipline: record the count before, prune, record it after, compute the difference. If the difference matches your expectation, the prune did its job. If it's smaller than expected, either the salience labels didn't reach below the floor or fewer records were in the store than you thought.

```scheme
;; Write a set of low-salience fillers, prune them, and verify the size dropped.
(let ((before (cortex/size)))
  (cortex/remember \"prune-test/f1\" \"ephemeral note\" :salience 0.1)
  (cortex/remember \"prune-test/f2\" \"another ephemeral note\" :salience 0.1)
  (cortex/remember \"prune-test/f3\" \"yet another ephemeral note\" :salience 0.1)
  (let ((after-writes (cortex/size)))
    ;; three fillers landed
    (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.15)
    (let ((after-prune (cortex/size)))
      ;; after-writes > before (we added three)
      ;; after-prune <= after-writes (we evicted some)
      (list (> after-writes before)
            (<= after-prune after-writes)))))
```

That returns `(#t #t)` — the writes grew the store, and the prune brought it back down. The size delta is the concrete proof the prune worked. In a real session where you're managing against a cap, this is the check you run after every significant sweep: verify the gauge dropped, then continue.

## A forget-then-recall-returns-`#f` cycle

`cortex/forget` is a scalpel, not a sweep. It removes exactly one key, and the proof it worked is that `cortex/recall` on that key immediately returns `#f`. Here's the cycle in full — write, confirm present, forget, confirm absent — so the behavior is unambiguous.

```scheme
;; Full forget cycle: write, verify present, forget, verify absent.
(begin
  ;; write a record under a known key
  (cortex/remember \"forget-test/target\"
    \"a fact about to be retired\" :salience 0.6)
  ;; confirm it's there
  (let ((before-forget (cortex/recall \"forget-test/target\")))
    ;; now forget it
    (let ((result (cortex/forget \"forget-test/target\")))
      ;; and confirm the recall now returns #f
      (let ((after-forget (cortex/recall \"forget-test/target\")))
        (list (if before-forget #t #f)   ; was present
              result                      ; forget returned #t
              (if after-forget #f #t)))))) ; now absent
```

That returns `(#t #t #t)` — the record was present, the forget returned `#t` (found it), and the recall now honestly returns `#f` (gone). The cycle proves that `forget` is exact: only the named key left, and the null is the clean signal of its departure.

## Why disk persistence changes the design

There is one fact about the Cortex that no API documentation can fully convey: the store does not reset between runs. This sounds obvious when you say it, but its implications are not obvious until you've built something that relies on cross-session memory. Here is why it changes the design.

Without disk persistence, every session starts from zero and ends at zero. The only state you can carry forward is what you've managed to encode in the program's logic. Memory is ephemeral — you might as well not have it. With disk persistence, a session that files a lesson is doing something permanent. The next run — an hour later, a week later, on a fresh terminal — finds exactly what was left there. This means the *first* time you pay for a lesson is the *last* time you pay for it in full. Every subsequent session cashes in on that first payment.

The practical consequence is that memory is worth investing in. A high-salience note written today is a shortcut for every future session that encounters the same question. A boundary fact filed once is never re-derived again. The prune cycle exists not because memory is cheap but because memory is permanent: you need to curate what persists, because what you file today stays until you explicitly retire it. A notebook that never closes needs to be kept in good order.

```scheme
;; Write a fact now and verify it persists — the disk keeps what you write.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"persist-demo/across-runs\"
    \"This fact was written and will outlive the current run.\" :salience 0.8)
  ;; in this same run, it's readable
  (string? (cortex/read \"persist-demo/across-runs\")))
```

Returns `#t` — and critically, if you were to run `(cortex/read \"persist-demo/across-runs\")` in a future invocation of the interpreter, you'd still get the string back. The read that proves the persistence is the *next* run's read, not this one — but the write that makes it possible is happening right now.

## Why salience, not LRU

A pure recency policy — keep the most recent `n` records and drop everything older — is simple and feels fair. But it fails the moment you have a fact that was established at the beginning of a session and has been referenced repeatedly since. Under a pure LRU, that fact ages out even though it's the most relevant thing in the store. Salience fixes this because salience is a *judgement*, not a clock reading.

When you write a lesson and assign it 0.9, you are saying: this is load-bearing; prioritize it in any squeeze. When you write a scratch note and leave it at the default 0.1, you are saying: disposable, let it go first. No clock knows the difference between those two; only your assessment of relevance does. The prune then executes that assessment mechanically: floor sweeps everything below the bar you set, in one pass, leaving exactly the records whose salience you deemed sufficient.

The one place where recency *does* matter is `cortex/topk`, which is explicitly a recency window — it gives you the most recent `k` records, newest first. But `topk` is a read operation, not an eviction one. It tells you what's been happening lately; it does not determine what survives a prune. The eviction axis is always salience. Recency tells you the recent history; salience tells you what matters enough to keep.

## Why the null is always `#f`, not an exception

The design choice to return `#f` from `cortex/read` and `cortex/recall` on a miss — rather than throwing an exception — reflects a philosophy about how boundary knowledge should feel. An exception is a crash that requires a handler; `#f` is a value that can be tested with `if`. The difference is the shape of the code you write at the call site.

With exceptions, you'd write:

```scheme
;; The #f design means you branch on the null, not catch an exception.
;; This is the intended pattern — not exception handling.
(let ((v (cortex/read \"maybe-there\")))
  (if v
      (string-append \"found: \" v)
      \"not there — and that is fine\"))
```

That returns either the found string or the acknowledgment that nothing was there. With `#f`, you handle the miss in the normal flow of the program, not in an error handler. This is deliberate: the miss is not exceptional; it is *one of two normal outcomes*. A key can be there or not. Both outcomes are regular. Building the calling code around a conditional is cleaner than building it around a try/catch, and it keeps the honest-null stance consistent: the `#f` is a value you process, not a crash you recover from.")
